welcome back so we're talking about data  science in this intro to data science  lecture series and I said in the past  that data science is largely about  asking and answering questions with data  so kind of the cycle of asking questions  and then trying to answer them with data  data-driven inquiry is is one way I like  to say it and I really want to dive into  what are the types of questions just  some of the like flavor of questions we  might ask with with data a lot of data  science kind of culminates in machine  learning where you build a model that  you can then deploy and use in the  future you can run hypotheses through it  you can run forecasting and scenarios  and you can use it in the field for  example to classify images or you train  a robot and you want it to go and be  autonomous and do something so so you a  lot of data science is culminating in  machine learning and models that you  will use in the future after training  them on data and so a major question  that I ask all the time in in data  science is does the past and so data  often is from the past does the past  represent the future and this can be  kind of that's a fairly vague statement  but I think you get what I mean  I have historical data often I've  collected a bunch of data can it  represent the future how can I use my  data to predict the future those are the  kinds of questions so does the data I've  collected do it does it have any bearing  on how I want to use my models in the  future the decisions I'm going to make  in the future at some point that asks  the begs the question what do I want a  model I have to - what do I want to  model it's not a beautiful sentence so I  have to ask myself what can I model what  would be useful to model  what do I want to model in the future so  in some cases it's pretty clear-cut  you know Facebook wants to be able to  take a picture of a face and tell who it  is  you know in an image search and Google  if there's a picture of a cat it would  love to be able to label what's in the  image or even what the image what's seen  it represents some cases is a little bit  less less obvious what you want to do so  you know what do I want to model in a  you know fluid flow well that requires  me to ask to do some hypothesis testing  of what I can model what would be  interesting but you always ask yourself  what do you want to model and a more  kind of we're just drilling down in  specifics here so does the past  represent the future what kinds of  things do I want a model how will the  model be used that's critically  important how will the model be used  who's going to use the model how is it  going to be used is it gonna have to be  very very fast is it gonna have to be  flexible and working scenarios that the  training data didn't didn't see you know  is it gonna have to generalize is it  who's gonna use the model is this model  gonna be used you know for something  that societally criticals it's going to  be used for you know the judicial system  and sentencing people based on  information because then you better be  able to explain the decisions you made  and things like that so how will the  model be used is safety critical is this  for an autonomous vehicle a self-driving  car okay so you have to know kind of  does the data I have represent the  future how what do I want to model who's  going to use the model on how and then  at this point it's critical to ask what  data do I need right so this is data  driven at some point so what data do I  need and a similar question is what data  do I have do I  it okay so what data do I need what they  did do I have  okay so we're asking questions about  what data we need and then you have to  ask how hard is it to get the data okay  and so this is just one kind of stream  of questions that you might be asking  that eventually culminate in kind of  data so now I have identified the  problem I want to model who's gonna use  it and how what data I'm going to need  so that the past will be useful in the  future  I know roughly how I'm gonna get the  data or at least I have an idea and then  I collect that data and I start trying  to build models and answer those  questions and so what I've pointed out  to you is kind of this path here where  the questions are somehow motivating the  data that I'm gonna collect so you know  sometimes someone just hands you data  and you mine it and see what you can get  out of it but really if you can be part  of the question asking the problem  solving procedure and again this is why  you want teams that have you know  problem-solving experts in data science  experts and you want your problem  solvers to know some data science and  your data scientists to know some  problem-solving because if you have the  ability to you know have this this pipe  line of questioning culminating in what  data to collect and how you're going to  store it and analyze it this tends to go  a lot better than if someone just hands  you data and says what's in it okay but  then you start analyzing the data and  what you might realize is that your  question wasn't the right question in  the first place  so if if you're lucky this will motivate  kind of a refinement of your questions  maybe maybe you decide that there's  something different you can model or  that you you can't model what you  thought maybe there's just not the  information in the data that you thought  there would be but that motivates a  different set of questioning a different  use case and so this is kind of a  feedback process and at multiple levels  you want this to feedback between  questions and data okay so for me that's  really the essence of  data science is asking and answering  questions with data all right thank you
 

